<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="chp1sec1" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    
        <title>Uniqueness of Identities and Inverses</title>
        <p>
            In this investigation, you now revisit basic algebraic properties of <m>\R</m>, properties you may have taken for granted, but you cannot do so for its abstraction. You will practice deducing these basis properties from field axioms.
        </p>
        <p>
            In <xref ref="def-field"/> we assume identity elements and inverses <em>exist</em>, we do not assume they are unique.
        </p>
        <proposition xml:id="prop-field-addititive_identity_uniqueness">
            <title>Additive identities are unique</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    If <m>F</m> is a field and <m>0_1,0_2\in F</m> both satisfy the property that, for all <m>a\in F</m>, <m>0_1+a=a</m> and <m>0_2+a=a</m>, then <m>0_1=0_2</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </proposition>
        <proof>
            <p>
                Try a direct proof. Analyze the sum <m>0_1+0_2</m>. You may find field property (F4) in <xref ref="def-field"/> useful.
            </p>
        </proof>
        <proposition>
            <title>Multiplicative identities are unique</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    If <m>F</m> is a field and <m>1_1,1_2\in F</m> both satisfy the property that, for all <m>a\in F</m>, <m>1_1a=a</m> and <m>1_2a=a</m>, then <m>1_1=1_2</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </proposition>
        <proof>
            <p>
                Have you seen a similar result whose proof you may leverage?
            </p>
        </proof>
        <p>
            We are therefore justified in discussing <em>the</em> identity element.
        </p>
        <proposition xml:id="prop-field-addititive_inverse_uniqueness">
            <title>Additive inverses are unique</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    If <m>F</m> is a field, <m>a\in F</m>, and <m>b,c\in F</m> satisfy the property that <m>a+b=0</m> and <m>a+c=0</m>, then <m>b=c</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </proposition>
        <proof>
            <p>
                Try a direct proof. Analyze the sum <m>b+a+c</m> in two ways. Remember, addition is a binary operation, so which pair do you add first? You may find field properties (F1), (F2) in <xref ref="def-field"/> useful.
            </p>
        </proof>
        <proposition>
            <title>Multiplicative inverses are unique</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    If <m>F</m> is a field, <m>a\in F^\times</m>, and <m>b,c\in F^\times</m> satisfy the property that <m>ab=1</m> and <m>ac=1</m>, then <m>b=c</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </proposition>
        <proof>
            <p>
                Have you seen a similar result whose proof you may leverage?
            </p>
        </proof>
        <p>
            We are therefore justified in discussing <em>the</em> inverse.  Going forward, we will use the notation <m>-a</m> to denote the additive inverse of <m>a\in F</m> and <m>a^{-1}</m> to denote the multiplicative inverse of <m>a\in F^\times</m>.
        </p>

</section>
